<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
{{!--
  Every relative URL in this page (logo, side-menu links, footer links, the
  module switcher — anything built with `{{relativize}}`) is computed as if
  the document lives at `/404.html` (relativize.ts: "a URL relative to the
  CURRENT page" — page.url on the synthetic 404 file is `/404.html`, see
  not-found-page.js). That's true for a real page: the browser is actually
  there. It is never true for a 404 — by definition, whatever URL served this
  page is NOT /404.html, and could be nested at any depth. Without a fixed
  base, the browser resolves each of those page-relative hrefs against the
  actual broken URL instead, and every one of them 404s again.

  `<base>` fixes all of them in one place, instead of hand-rewriting every
  partial the 404 layout happens to pull in (header, side menu, site footer,
  and whatever else grows to use `{{relativize}}` later): it retargets what
  "relative to the current page" MEANS for the rest of this document, to the
  site root — the one location that's true regardless of which broken URL
  got here. `site.path` is the site's own root when it's deployed under a
  path prefix (see relativize.ts's own use of it); empty for a root
  deployment, giving `/`.
--}}
    <base href="{{site.path}}/">
{{> head defaultPageTitle='Page Not Found'}}
  </head>
  <body class="status-404">
{{> header}}
{{> body}}
{{!--
  The site footer (brand, authored link columns, modules, copyright), not
  the page footer (footer.hbs): that one's pagination row assumes a page
  inside a reading sequence, and its feedback block ("Help us make these
  docs great!") assumes there is a page to propose a change to — neither is
  true of a 404. Same footer, and the same reasoning, as home.hbs.
--}}
{{> site-footer}}
{{> footer-scripts}}
  </body>
</html>
